# Videotape
#
# Magnetic tape for pictures, from two-inch quadruplex in 1956 through U-matic, Betamax, VHS, Video8, and DV, and the helical scan that let small cassettes hold television.

#>media_carriers_videotape
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!media_carriers
#:form #!timeline
#:level #!foundation
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bonk
#:era #!modern

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Videotape"
 ~#claim: "Video needs far more bandwidth than audio, so recorders spin heads across the tape; helical scan made that small enough for cassettes, from U-matic in 1971 to DV in 1995."
 ~#proof: "Abramson for broadcast and industrial machines; Lardner for the home VCR; IEC 61834 for DV; a Wikipedia cross-check for helical scan origins."
 ~#door: "Wrap paper around a can to see a helical track, then climb the ladder from quad to DV."
 ~#audience: "readers with boxes of old tapes, media history students, and archivists"
 continuity: "Videotape | spin heads across the tape"
 cold_open: "Pictures need so much bandwidth that video recorders spin heads across the tape, and helical scan shrank that into a cassette."
}

